  "query_preview": "periods of time outdoors, especially if exercising. An \nExtreme Heat Warning has been issued from Saturday afternoon \nthrough Sunday morning for this area of greatest heat risk. The \nTwin Cities metro also has an Extreme Heat Warning starting the \nsame time on Saturday and continuing all the way through Monday \nmorning. The hot and humid conditions spread eastward Sunday and\nMonday, though with the uncertainties, we have kept everywhere \noutside of the metro with an Extreme Heat Watch for now. The \nmetro will stay warm overnight (mid to upper 70s) such that \nconfidence remains high for prolonged impacts from the heat. The\nheat will continue into next week, but dewpoints and air \ntemperatures will be about 5-8 degrees cooler than this weekend.\n\nThunderstorm Chances...Several weak disturbances are expected to \ninitiate a few rounds of thunderstorms, starting this evening \nacross eastern ND and northern MN. Most CAMs have focused in on \na similar solution showing scattered storms developing late \nafternoon and progressing eastern through the evening. The \nmajority of the convection likely will fall apart by the time it\nreaches central MN given the lack of instability across the \narea and warming mid- levels. That said, there is still a \nmarginal SWO for portions of central MN with the low end \nscenario of a storm or two continuing further south than \nexpected late this evening.\n\nStorms will likely continue moving east across northern MN and \nnorthern WI through the morning, and additional isolated storm \ndevelopment will be possible along a boundary draped through \nwestern WI and east-central MN. \n\nA fairly broad Day 2 Marginal SWO was introduced across much of\nWI and eastern MN, highlighting the chance for isolated \nredevelopment tomorrow afternoon. Eastern WI would be the \nfavored region for any storm initiation due to weaker capping \neast of the thermal ridge. Much of MN could be well-capped with \n700 mb temperatures expected to be up to +12-15C. \n\nSunday could end up being a very similar situation with overall \nstorm coverage remaining dependent on the extent of our thermal \nprofile. There will be a slightly more favorable environment for\nany storms to do form to become severe, as highlighted by the \nDay 3 Slight risk.\n\nAdditional storm chances will be possible into next week, though\nit will continue to rely on this smaller scale disturbances \nwithin the upper level ridge.",
